--TEST--
A Core\Ast\Node's column is in characters and its offset is in bytes, so two lines differing only in character width agree on the column — `rule:core-classes/ast-is-inert`, `rule:ide/positions-have-one-home`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The *agreement* shape, over the one question the three position members
// answer differently: `column()` counts characters and `offset()` counts
// bytes. Two statements are written with the same shape and the same number of
// characters, one of them holding a two-byte `ä` and a three-byte `€`, and the
// assertion is that the columns agree while the byte distances do not — a
// column computed from the offset alone reads right on the ASCII line and puts
// every later column on the wide one past where the text is.
var $src = "<?nvs\necho \"ae\", 7;\necho \"ä€\", 7;\n";
var $tree = Core\Ast::parse($src);
var $walk = $tree->nodes();

// Each line's `echo` is at column 1, so its offset is the line's own start —
// which is what turns an offset into a byte column below without this case
// counting the source a second time.
var $plainEcho = $walk[0];
var $plainInt = $walk[2];
var $wideEcho = $walk[3];
var $wideInt = $walk[5];

int $plainStart = $plainEcho->offset() - ($plainEcho->column() - 1);
int $wideStart = $wideEcho->offset() - ($wideEcho->column() - 1);

echo "columns ", $plainInt->column(), " ", $wideInt->column(), "\n";
echo "byte columns ", $plainInt->offset() - $plainStart + 1, " ",
    $wideInt->offset() - $wideStart + 1, "\n";
echo $plainInt->column() == $wideInt->column() ? "the same column on both lines" : "the column moved with the bytes", "\n";
echo "bytes the two characters add: ",
    ($wideInt->offset() - $wideStart) - ($plainInt->offset() - $plainStart), "\n";

// And the lines themselves are counted from 1, one apart, so nothing above is
// two readings of the same line.
echo $plainInt->line(), " ", $wideInt->line(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
columns 12 12
byte columns 12 15
the same column on both lines
bytes the two characters add: 3
2 3
